#{[17592186272285 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expressions are ${R}^{n}$ and $n.$"] [17592186272291 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$ is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$ is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$ only."] [17592186315028 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,5),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,5), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-5,0).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-5,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(3(-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186315030 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2(-x)^{3}+3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}-3x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186315064 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,8),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,8), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-8,0).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-8,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-(-x)^{2}+5(-x)) \\\\  & =-(-{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}+5(-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186315070 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn this case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186326932 "Notice that we have  a block upper triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & b & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =\\left|\\begin{array}{cc|c}a & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & b & 12 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & 2 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329634 "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & d & e \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\left|\\begin{array}{c|cc}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & d & e \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{array}\\right| \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186348410 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/d2/d2b5c55b15f594680e8f299899d690cd1d92baa1ebef31ce5c79af52afec631e.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ \\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$ Since $D∖R$ is empty, the function $f(x,y)$ will be identical to zero for all points in $R,$ including $D.$ But $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ is not zero, for example, at $(x,y)=(0,2)\\in D.$\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equals $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [17592186354529 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-1,2]\\times [0,1]$ and $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,1]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ only."] [17592186354530 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/62/62925844de27c9731b181f17a1bdd912bfa3265176bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$” only."] [17592186354532 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/23/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$ The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [17592186354533 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/f3/f3ffc82025f80c357c06e28fcb091c701d53d84821bebd9d2886cf4972b77028.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$ The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [17592186360757 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.85)}^{x-1}(0.15), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=7) & =f(7) \\\\  & ={(0.85)}^{6}(0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.0566.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360760 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.23),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.77)}^{x-1}(0.23), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<7) & =P(X\\in \\{4,5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(0.77)}^{3}(0.23)+{(0.77)}^{4}(0.23)+{(0.77)}^{5}(0.23) \\\\  & ≈0.2481.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364145 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ only."] [17592186367401 "Notice that we have  a block lower triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3q & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & p\\end{vmatrix} & =\\left|\\begin{array}{c|cc}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 1 & 3q & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & p\\end{array}\\right| \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3q & -6 \\\\ 2 & p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-40\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367408 "Notice that we have  a block lower triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}p & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ r & 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc|c}p & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\hline r & 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{array}\\right| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}p & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186367971 "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & b & 0 \\\\ d & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =\\left|\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & b & 0 \\\\ d & 2 & 0 \\\\ \\hline 0 & 0 & -4\\end{array}\\right| \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & b \\\\ d & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186379568 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+4(-x)-9) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-4x-9) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+4x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186379595 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,12),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,12), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-12,0).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-12,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-6(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-(-4)^{2}-6(-4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186380838 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/77/77c8e1b1aacd29c6d69aa3c641c1641f43ff2aa1467dc713d417641f8b76b066.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$ Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equals ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [17592186380845 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/38/38c44f897999039f88df2c3e549a1229ec9f9a979bbc83dfe1a5d7279701450d.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y{e}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equal $y{e}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$ The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(3,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [17592186382400 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/5c/5c5dbc673c2c6a9bd0a4f06223133e21e2dcbbdbaa2dfe2de2b29d8824dd821e.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$” are true. In both cases, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$” only."] [17592186398726 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$"] [17592186399685 "Consider the line $x+2y=-8.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x-4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}x-4,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+2y\\ge -8.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\ge -8 \\\\ y\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186401928 "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186403037 "Consider the line $y=3x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 3x+1.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186403044 "Consider the line $y=2x+6.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<2x+6.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=5.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y<5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186404461 "Consider the line $x+y=3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-x+3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\"  inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+3,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+y>3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=2x.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict.  Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y>3 \\\\ y>2x.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186404468 "Consider the line $y=4x+3.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<4x+3.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{4}x-2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y<4x+3 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186404475 "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186407297 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$” only."] [17592186409889 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $-4$ and $0$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{-4,0\\}.$"] [17592186409892 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $b,c$ and $e$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{b,c,e\\}.$"] [17592186410428 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor $x=-5,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 0]=1[-5\\ge 0]=0$ (since $-5\\ge 0$ is false)\n\n- $1[-3\\le x<0]=1[-3\\le -5<0]=0$ (since $-3\\le -5$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-3]=1[-5<-3]=1$ (since $-5<-3$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $p(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(-5) & =0\\cdot 25+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2(-5)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-5<-3,$ only the third term contributes to the result."] [17592186413692 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2,4,6$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2),$ $(2,4),$ and $(2,6),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(4,4),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $2$nd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=6$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,6),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |"] [17592186413694 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(✠,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $✠$-row and the $♣$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♣,♠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♣$-row and the $♠$-column.\n\n- The pair $(♠,✠)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $♠$-row and the $✠$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(✠,♣)$ only."] [17592186413695 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=0,6,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,0),$ $(1,6),$ and $(1,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=0,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,0)$ and $(4,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $1$st column and in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,0),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |"] [17592186413696 "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(4,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $4$-row and the $3$-column.\n\n- The pair $(8,6)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $8$-row and the $6$-column.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $2$-row and the $3$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(8,6)$; $(2,3)$ only."] [17592186419955 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0\\le x\\le 6,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{108}t(t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{324}{t}^{2}(t+3)|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186420184 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}{e}^{(-3)^{2}}=9{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186420398 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{m,r,t\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1fd2100d57c3483e737dc40298d4d5f61099ebc2863c54839906b66183acf576.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $m,r$ and $t$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$"] [17592186420399 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{2,4,8\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/85/850019897d92b0a8d3340c091cee15342be20e99e7578a9fca23f88804e3bf28.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $2,4$ and $8$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$"] [17592186423942 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\},$ and the set $S=\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=0,1,2,3,4,5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ there are $2$ items $(0,1)$ that satisfy $X<2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<2)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186423943 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{3,7,9,10\\},$ and the set $S=\\{3,7,9,10\\}$ has $|S|=4$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}, & x=3,7,9,10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=9)=f(9)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186423944 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{9,10,11,…,17\\},$ and the set $S=\\{9,10,11,…,17\\}$ has $|S|=17-9+1=9$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}, & x=9,10,11,…,17 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<12$ are $9,10,11.$ There are $3$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<12)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186423945 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,3,…,17\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,3,…,17\\}$ has $|S|=17-1+1=17$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{17}, & x=1,2,3,…,17 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ there are $7$ items $(2,3,4,5,6,7,8)$ that satisfy $2\\le X<9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(2\\le X<9)=7\\cdot \\frac{1}{17}=\\frac{7}{17}.\n$$"] [17592186423946 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{11,13,14,16,20\\},$ and the set $S=\\{11,13,14,16,20\\}$ has $|S|=5$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & x=11,13,14,16,20 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ there are $2$ items $(13,14)$ that satisfies $13\\le X<16.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(13\\le X<16)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186423947 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{13,14,18\\},$ and the set $S=\\{13,14,18\\}$ has $|S|=3$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=13,14,18 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=14)=f(14)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186424000 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(1).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X\\ge 2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424001 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-3x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-3x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x/4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3/4}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.5276\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424002 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(1\\le X\\le 3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6}+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1329\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424004 "An exponential random variable $U$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda u}, & u\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nU∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2u}, & u\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(U\\ge 3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U\\ge 3) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2u}{|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-6} \\\\  & ≈0.0025\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424050 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-3,1],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}, & -3\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 2) & =P(-2\\le X\\le 1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424051 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $W$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le w\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[0,4],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 0\\le w\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\ge 1) & =P(1\\le W\\le 4) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424052 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $U$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le u\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $U$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-10,10],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20},\\, & -10\\le u\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U<-3) & =P(-10\\le U<-3) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-10}^{-3}f(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-10}^{-3}\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-(-10)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424053 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $W$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le w\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[3,10],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7},\\, & 3\\le w\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\le 5) & =P(3\\le W\\le 5) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{5-3}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424054 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\pi },\\, & -\\pi \\le x\\le \\pi , \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-0)}{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424143 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $3\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{2}{3}(t-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{3}t(t-4)|}_{3}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}x(x-4)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+3}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-4x+3}{3}, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186424144 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186424145 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 0,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{20}{e}^{-t/20}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{-t/20}{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/20}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-x/20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ 1-{e}^{-x/20}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186424374 "Let $X$ represent the number of the app picked. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X>16).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,21\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,21\\}$ has $|S|=21-4+1=18$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}, & x=4,5,…,21 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X>16$ are $17,18,19,20,21.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>16)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}=\\frac{5}{18}.\n$$"] [17592186424376 "Let $X$ represent the issue Jack selects. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{4,5,…,79\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X=20\\,\\,or\\,\\,X=23).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{4,5,…,79\\},$ and the set $S=\\{4,5,…,79\\}$ has $|S|=79-4+1=76$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{76}, & x=4,5,…,79 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy the given condition are $20,23.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=20\\,\\,or\\,\\,X=23)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{76}=\\frac{1}{38}.\n$$"] [17592186424378 "Let $X$ represent the number on which the die lands. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,6\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,…,6\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=1,2,…,6 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\in \\{2,3,5\\}$ are $2,3,5.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\in \\{2,3,5\\})=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186424379 "Let $X$ represent the parking space Emma picks. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{215,216,…,268\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(234\\le X\\le 245).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{215,216,…,268\\},$ and the set $S=\\{215,216,…,268\\}$ has $|S|=268-215+1=54$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{54}, & x=215,216,…,268 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $234\\le X\\le 245$ are $234,235,…,245.$ There are $245-234+1=12$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(234\\le X\\le 245)=12\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$"] [17592186424573 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X>\\sqrt{2})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>\\sqrt{2}) & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\infty }\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}x}{|}_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424575 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-x/5}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X\\le 2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-x/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{{e}^{-x/5}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/5}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.3297\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424576 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\sqrt{3}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{3})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{1}{3}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\sqrt{3}{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}x}{|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\sqrt{3}/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.4386\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424577 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(2<X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<3) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/3}{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.1455\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424579 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(8).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-8x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X>0.4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>0.4) & ={\\int }_{0.4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0.4}^{\\infty }8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-8x}{|}_{0.4}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-3.2} \\\\  & ≈0.0408\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424580 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(1).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(0.5\\le X\\le 2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le X\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0.5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0.5}^{2}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}{|}_{0.5}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{-0.5} \\\\  & ≈0.4712\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186424886 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186424888 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}(t-1)(t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}({t}^{2}-5t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2}{27}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{t}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x-(-\\frac{2}{27}+\\frac{5}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186425929 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/ce/ce9b9f3435a94b3f9f87b195e5b1591c80281b23cfb76ceb8e79cdeec3ae66b0.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$ Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equals $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [17592186425930 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,1]\\times [-3,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” only."] [17592186425931 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3b/3bfc135232008824ae0522bcc8454c5f97c2dfd13fb6e333e146a040ccbc1f22.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+3y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,2]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,2]$” only."] [17592186426267 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this problem, the break point $X$ is uniformly distributed over the interval $[0,1]$, so the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nWhen the stick is broken at point $X$, it is divided into two pieces: one of length $X$ and the other of length $1-X.$ The longer of the two pieces, $Y,$ is therefore given by\n\n$$\nY=max(X,1-X).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we define\n\n$$\nr(x)=max(x,1-x).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, we express $r(x)$ as an explicit piecewise function. Observe that,\n\n- if $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$, then $x\\le 1-x$, so the longer piece has length $1-x,$\n\n- if $\\frac{1}{2}<x\\le 1$, then $x>1-x$, so the longer piece has length $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ x, & \\,\\frac{1}{2}<x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[max(X,1-X)] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(1-x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/2}+{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is $\\frac{3}{4}.$"] [17592186427243 "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x)$, the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(x)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\text{ }\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability density function of $X.$ \n\nIn this problem, the break point $X$ is chosen uniformly from $[0,4]$, so the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\le x<4.\n$$\n\nWhen the stick is broken at point $X$, it is divided into two pieces: one of length $X$ and the other of length $4-X.$ The longer of the two pieces, $Y$, is therefore given by\n\n$$\nY=\\text{max}(X,4-X).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we define\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\text{max}(x,4-x).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, we express $r(x)$ as an explicit piecewise function. Observe that,\n\n- if $0\\le x\\le 2$, then $x\\le 4-x$, so the longer piece has length $4-x$,\n\n- if $2<x\\le 4$, then $x>4-x$, so the longer piece has length $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4-x, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ x, & 2<x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[\\text{max}(X,4-X)] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[4x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8-2)+\\frac{1}{4}(8-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4}+\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is $3.$"] [17592186434097 "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{96}\\cdot 33.86574 \\\\  & ≈0.3528\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
